--TEST--
Core\Jwt::verify accepts a token at every moment of its one-second lifetime and refuses it in the very next second, and the expiry refusal is the one failure with its own sentence — `rule:security/protocol-roster` and `rule:security/algorithm-comes-from-the-key`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// A bound asserted on both sides. `exp` counts whole seconds (RFC 7519
// § 4.1.4), which is why `Core\Jwt::sign` refuses a lifetime shorter than one
// — and it is also what makes the bound reachable from a test at all: the last
// second a token is accepted in is `iat`, the first it is refused in is `exp`,
// and they are adjacent. A member comparing `>=` where it means `>` prints
// plausibly against either half of this on its own.
secret bytes $key = Core\Crypto::generateKey();

// Start at a second boundary, so the one-second lifetime below is signed at
// the beginning of its own second rather than at an unknown point inside it.
// Nothing here reads a clock twice and assumes it did not move; the two loops
// are written against the second they want to be in.
int $before = Core\Time::now()->toEpochSeconds();
while (Core\Time::now()->toEpochSeconds() == $before) {
    Core\Time::sleep(5ms);
}

string $token = Core\Jwt::sign(["sub" => "ada"], 1s, $key);
array<tainted string> $issued = Core\Jwt::verify($token, $key);
int $iat = $issued["iat"] as int;
int $exp = $issued["exp"] as int;
if ($exp - $iat == 1) {
    echo "the shortest lifetime this member accepts is one whole second", "\n";
}

// The accepted side, counted rather than sampled once: every reading taken
// while the clock is still inside `iat` verifies. A member that expired the
// token early fails here while still refusing it in the next second, which is
// the half a single check after signing cannot see.
int $checks = 0;
int $verified = 0;
int $refusedEarly = 0;
string $earlySays = "";
while (Core\Time::now()->toEpochSeconds() < $exp) {
    $checks = $checks + 1;
    try {
        array<tainted string> $still = Core\Jwt::verify($token, $key);
        $verified = $verified + 1;
    } catch (RuntimeError $early) {
        $refusedEarly = $refusedEarly + 1;
        $earlySays = $early->message;
    }
    Core\Time::sleep(50ms);
}
if ($checks == $verified && $refusedEarly == 0 && $checks > 0) {
    echo "it verifies at every moment of the second it was issued in", "\n";
} else {
    echo "it stopped verifying inside its own lifetime: ", $earlySays, "\n";
}

// The refused side, in `exp` itself and not one second later — the loop waits
// for that exact second rather than sleeping a fixed amount and hoping.
while (Core\Time::now()->toEpochSeconds() < $exp) {
    Core\Time::sleep(5ms);
}
string $expiredSays = "";
if (Core\Time::now()->toEpochSeconds() == $exp) {
    try {
        array<tainted string> $late = Core\Jwt::verify($token, $key);
        echo "a token verified in the second it expired in", "\n";
    } catch (RuntimeError $expired) {
        $expiredSays = $expired->message;
        echo "and in the very next second it is refused", "\n";
    }
}

// And that refusal is the deliberate exception to the one-sentence rule: it is
// reached only after the signature has been checked, so it may say what is
// wrong. Compared against the sentence a forgery gets — the module doc's
// *verification returns claims or throws* section owns why exactly one of
// these two is allowed to be specific.
string $forgedSays = "";
try {
    array<tainted string> $forged = Core\Jwt::verify($token . "Z", $key);
    echo "an altered signature verified", "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $notOurs) {
    $forgedSays = $notOurs->message;
}
if ($expiredSays != $forgedSays && Core\Str::contains($expiredSays, "expired at")) {
    echo "an expired token is told so; every other failure is one sentence", "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
the shortest lifetime this member accepts is one whole second
it verifies at every moment of the second it was issued in
and in the very next second it is refused
an expired token is told so; every other failure is one sentence
